# Enterprise code

Write code a new engineer (and a new Cursor session) can understand without explanation. Prefer structure over cleverness.

## Structure

- Clear module boundaries. One responsibility per file and module.
- Place code where `architecture/` says it lives. New placement → update architecture in the same turn.
- Small, named functions and types. No nested anonymous logic at boundaries.
- Shared constants only when used in more than one file.

## Clarity

- Names describe intent (`getActiveLeaseById`, not `getData` / `handle`).
- Explicit types at public boundaries. No unexplained `any`.
- Linear, obvious control flow. Fail explicitly; do not swallow errors.
- No comments that narrate the code. Comment only non-obvious constraints.

## Change discipline

- Smallest change that solves the problem. No drive-by refactors or unused abstractions.
- Match existing patterns in the same layer. A new pattern needs an ADR in `architecture/decisions/`.
- Do not add unused files, exports, or dependencies.

## Example

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
export async function handle(req: any) {
  const d = await db.query("...");
  return d;
}

// ✅ GOOD
export async function getLeaseById(id: LeaseId): Promise<Lease> {
  return leaseRepository.findById(id);
}
```
